-- title:
--   The anemic weight-2 Hecke algebra is reduced
-- statement:
--   Let $N$ be a natural number that is nonzero and let $S \subseteq \mathbb{N}$ be a set of natural numbers such that every prime $q$ dividing $N$ belongs to $S$. Consider the space $S_2(\Gamma_0(N))$ of weight-$2$ cusp forms for the congruence subgroup $\Gamma_0(N)$, viewed as a complex vector space, and inside its $\mathbb{C}$-linear endomorphism ring the generating set [`CuspForm.heckeGenerators`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L14) consisting of the operators $T_\ell$ attached (via `heckeTLin`, in weight $2$) to primes $\ell$ with $\ell \nmid N$ and $\ell \notin S$, together with the operators $U_q$ attached (via `heckeULin`) to primes $q$ with $q \mid N$ and $q \notin S$; the hypothesis on $S$ makes the second family empty, so only the $T_\ell$ with $\ell \nmid N$, $\ell \notin S$ occur. The assertion is that the Hecke algebra [`CuspForm.heckeAlgebra N 2 S`](def/CuspForm_HeckeAlgebra.html#L18), namely the $\mathbb{Z}$-subalgebra of $\operatorname{End}_{\mathbb{C}}(S_2(\Gamma_0(N)))$ generated by that set, is a reduced ring: its only nilpotent element is $0$. The weight is fixed to be the integer $2$, and no further restriction is placed on $S$ beyond containing the prime divisors of $N$.
--
--   This is the classical semisimplicity, away from the level, of the Hecke algebra acting on weight-$2$ cusp forms: the operators $T_\ell$ with $\ell \nmid N$ are simultaneously diagonalisable on the finite-dimensional space $S_2(\Gamma_0(N))$, so the ring they generate has no nilpotents; the hypothesis that $S$ contains the primes dividing $N$ excludes the $U_q$, which can act nilpotently on oldforms. Reducedness is used downstream when passing to localisations of the Hecke algebra at a maximal ideal and attaching Galois representations to them, and it is cited in the construction of the Hecke Galois representation data and in the results on points of the localised Hecke algebra.
